arXiv:2605.07994v1 [cs.IT] 08 May 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haricharan Balasundaram, Swathi Shree Narashiman, Pranay Mathur, Andrew Thangaraj
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Electrical Engineering, IIT Madras
<br class="ltx_break">Chennai, India
<br class="ltx_break">Email: haricharanb,swathinarashiman,pranaym@smail.iitm.ac.in, andrew@ee.iitm.ac.in
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose semantic smoothing, a smoothing method for language models that uses embeddings to share statistical observations across semantically similar contexts. The starting point is a decomposition of log-perplexity that motivates smoothing as a collection of distribution-estimation problems under Kullback-Leibler (KL) loss. We then show that, under a Lipschitz-logit model for embedding-based language generation, proximity of context embeddings implies proximity of the corresponding next-word distributions in KL divergence. Combining these observations, we formulate semantic smoothing as distribution estimation in KL loss with KL-proximity side information. For <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> samples on a <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-symbol alphabet with a side-information distribution at KL distance <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, we give an interpolation estimator with worst-case KL risk <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\min\{\Delta,d/n\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\min\{\Delta,d/n\})</annotation></semantics></math>, and prove a matching-order lower bound for uniform side information. We extend the estimator to multiple and empirically estimated synonymous distributions. Experiments on synthetic Markov data and WikiText-103 bigram models using Word2Vec, GloVe, and GPT-2 embeddings show that semantic smoothing consistently reduces test perplexity when applied to add-constant and Kneser–Ney estimates.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>language models, smoothing, distribution estimation, KL divergence, embeddings, perplexity

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram language model posits a conditional distribution <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Pr}(w_{k}|w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(w_{k}|w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math> for the next word <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> given the <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-1</annotation></semantics></math> preceding words or context <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},w_{2},\dots,w_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is learned using a training corpus of sentences minimizing a metric called <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">perplexity</span> (a modified version of log-loss that will be precisely defined later) and is expected to maintain low perplexity on a new test corpus of sentences. Since the test sentences will contain <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-grams unseen in the training corpus, traditional Maximum Likelihood (ML) estimates result in unacceptably large test perplexity and <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smoothing</span>, which is the procedure for assigning suitable nonzero probabilities to unseen <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-grams, becomes a very important requirement for language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Smoothing methods for language models include i) add-constant smoothing studied by Laplace <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and Lidstone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, and the interpolation/backoff-based ones proposed by ii) Katz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, iii) Jelinek-Mercer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and iv) Kneser-Ney <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. The interpolation/backoff-based methods use <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-gram frequency counts for <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\ldots,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\ldots,k</annotation></semantics></math> and combine them using natural language heuristics. Kneser-Ney smoothing results in the lowest test perplexities in several data sets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. However, these models incur very high complexity as <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases and, in recent years, they have been superseded in performance and popularity by large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> that use embeddings of words over a large context to train a <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">transformed</span> context embedding, which translates to a conditional next-word distribution estimate. It is well known that LLMs require very large data sets and significant compute power for training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose a smoothing principle that uses semantic similarity, as measured by embeddings, to share probability mass across contexts. Classical smoothing methods share information across lower-order contexts or frequency classes. In contrast, semantic smoothing shares information across contexts whose embeddings are close, and therefore whose next-word distributions are expected to be close. We justify this principle in three steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">1) <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Perplexity decomposition</span>: We observe that log-perplexity for a fixed context length decomposes into a model-independent, empirical conditional entropy of the test sequence plus a weighted sum of Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergences. So, minimization of empirical perplexity is achieved by minimizing the KL loss of distribution estimation after each context independently.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">2) <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Word embeddings</span>: We determine semantic similarity of contexts through the proximity of their embeddings. We show that under suitable Lipschitz-logit conditions (satisfied by popular models such as Word2Vec <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and GPT-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>) embedding proximity translates to a closeness of conditional next-word distributions in KL distance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">3) <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic smoothing</span>: Combining the above two ideas, we propose semantic smoothing of language models. Semantically similar contexts (such as the synonyms ‘enormous’ and ‘big’ in bigram models) result in similar conditional distributions that can be combined to result in improved test perplexities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed semantic smoothing essentially reduces the problem of smoothing to a distribution estimation problem under KL loss given the knowledge that the unknown distribution is within a known KL distance of a side information distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Our main theoretical result is as follows. For the underlying problem of distribution estimation with <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples over a size-<math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> alphabet under KL loss with a known side information distribution at a proximity of <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in KL distance, we provide a minimax upper bound of <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\min(\Delta,d/n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\min(\Delta,d/n))</annotation></semantics></math> through an <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interpolation</span> estimator that linearly interpolates between the add-constant estimator and the side information distribution with suitable weights. In the case where the side information distribution is uniform over the alphabet, we show a matching minimax lower bound using Assouad’s lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For experiments, we consider bigram semantic smoothing, where, for each context word <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, we determine <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> synonyms as the closest words <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{1},\ldots,w_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1},\ldots,w_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> in the embedding space and interpolate between the conditional distribution estimates <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Pr}_{\text{train}}(\cdot|w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Pr</mtext><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}_{\text{train}}(\cdot|w)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p9.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Pr}_{\text{train}}(\cdot|w_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Pr</mtext><mtext>train</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}_{\text{train}}(\cdot|w_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,m</annotation></semantics></math>, with suitable weights as suggested by the theory. We evaluate the perplexity of our proposed bigram semantic smoothing method in the following scenarios: (1) synthetic Markov data with a low-rank factorization for the log of the transition matrix resulting in state embeddings, (2) WikiText-103 with Word2Vec/GloVe embeddings in a bigram model, and (3) WikiText-103 with GPT-2 embeddings in a bigram model of tokens<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
          The code for the experiments can be found at <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">https://github.com/BaristaBandits/Distribution_Estimation</span>.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Related work</span>: Outside of the classical smoothing methods mentioned earlier, recent work on <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-gram smoothing includes Falahatgar et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> that studies Kneser–Ney smoothing through competitive distribution estimation, and Malagutti et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> that relates <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-gram smoothing to regularization methods for neural language models. Our work is closest in spirit to this statistical line, but differs in that we formulate semantic smoothing as distribution estimation under KL loss with KL-proximity side information and prove minimax upper and lower bounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">A second related direction is retrieval- and nearest-neighbor-based language modeling. The <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>NN-LM of Khandelwal et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> interpolates a neural language model with a distribution induced by nearest neighbors in representation space, and subsequent work studies why such nearest-neighbor language models improve perplexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. These methods, like ours, use representation-space proximity to improve next-token prediction. However, our focus is different: rather than retrieving token-level neighbors at inference time, we use embedding proximity between contexts to construct semantically smoothed conditional distributions, with interpolation weights motivated by KL-risk bounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Distribution estimation with side information is a framework introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> in the <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>–norm. The technical extensions in this work include KL loss with KL proximity (which has technical challenges especially in the lower bound), and the practical scenario where the synonymous distribution is to be estimated from the samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">LLM alignment given a prompt is an important problem studied, for instance, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, wherein the expected reward function has to be maximized subject to a KL–divergence constraint. This problem is mathematically similar to our distribution estimation with side information framework, with a difference that LLM alignment requires the final estimate to be at a distance <math id="S1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> from a known distribution (the constraint) as opposed to <math id="S1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> being the separation between the two synonymous distributions themselves.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p14" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Organization of the Paper</span>: We introduce notation and state our results on perplexity decomposition, closeness of word embeddings, and i.i.d. distribution estimation with side information, respectively in Sections <a href="#S2" title="II Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, <a href="#S3" title="III Embeddings, Semantic Similarity and Proximity in KL Distance ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, and <a href="#S4" title="IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. We explain how we combine these results to get semantic smoothing for a language model in Section <a href="#S5" title="V Semantic Smoothing: Theory to Practice ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> and provide experiments on synthetic and natural language data in Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Experiments ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a>. We provide concluding remarks in Section <a href="#S7" title="VII Concluding remarks ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> and proofs for the theorems in Section <a href="#S8" title="VIII Proofs of Theorems ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a sequence of words <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{n}=(W_{1},W_{2},\ldots,W_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{n}=(W_{1},W_{2},\ldots,W_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> from natural language text to be a random process. The perplexity of a given word sequence <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{n}=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{n}=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{PPL}(w^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{PPL}(w^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{PPL}(w^{n})=\exp\left(-\frac{1}{n}\log\text{Pr}(W^{n}=w^{n})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mtext>Pr</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{PPL}(w^{n})=\exp\left(-\frac{1}{n}\log\text{Pr}(W^{n}=w^{n})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>log</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log</annotation></semantics></math> function is to the base <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> unless mentioned otherwise. This true perplexity is not computable because <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Pr}(W^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(W^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is unknown and can only be estimated approximately. A popular approximation stems from the <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram assumption.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><math id="S2.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-gram language models</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The one-sided <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram context of the word <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the length-<math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k{-}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k{-}1)</annotation></semantics></math> random vector <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i,k}(W^{n})=(W_{i-k+1},W_{i-k+2},\dots,W_{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i,k}(W^{n})=(W_{i-k+1},W_{i-k+2},\dots,W_{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to be a special start-of-sentence word. Under the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram assumption, we suppose that the word sequence is a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k{-}1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k{-}1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th order stationary Markov process, i.e. <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> given the context <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i,k}(W^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i,k}(W^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is conditionally independent of all other words. So, under <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram, the probability of a given word sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{n}=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{n}=(w_{1},\ldots,w_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Pr}(W^{n}=w^{n})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\text{Pr}(W_{i}{=}w_{i}|C_{i}{=}c_{i})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(w_{i}|c_{i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Pr}(W^{n}=w^{n})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\text{Pr}(W_{i}{=}w_{i}|C_{i}{=}c_{i})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(w_{i}|c_{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{i}=C_{i,k}(W^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{i}=C_{i,k}(W^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}=c_{i,k}(w^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}=c_{i,k}(w^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> are shorthand notations and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\cdot|c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\cdot|c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transition probability of the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-th order Markov process for the context <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The perplexity of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> under the <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram assumption with transition probability <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{PPL}(p,w^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{PPL}(p,w^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, is seen to be</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textsf{PPL}(p,w^{n})=\exp\left(-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log p(w_{i}|c_{i})\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">PPL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{PPL}(p,w^{n})=\exp\left(-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\log p(w_{i}|c_{i})\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">A <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram language model learns or estimates the transition probability <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> for a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram context <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> using a training corpus, which is a sequence of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> words that we will denote <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. For a particular language model <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote the estimated transition probability as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> or simply <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m30" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{p}_{M}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{M}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math>, when the training corpus is not important.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Test perplexity and its decomposition</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The figure of merit for a language model <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is its <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">test</span> perplexity, i.e. <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{PPL}(\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}},v^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{PPL}(\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}},v^{T})</annotation></semantics></math> of a test sequence of words <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{T}=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{T}=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is different from the train sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. A good model should have low test perplexity, which requires smoothing, i.e. assigning nonzero probabilities to contexts and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-grams unseen in the training corpus <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and likely to be seen in the test sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a sequence <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram context <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and a word <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, let</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{w^{n},k}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{w^{n},k}(c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}I(c_{i,k}(w^{n})=c)\text{ and }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}I(c_{i,k}(w^{n})=c)\text{ and }</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{w^{n},k}(w|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{w^{n},k}(w|c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}I(c_{i,k}(w^{n})=c)I(w_{i}=w)}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}I(c_{i,k}(w^{n})=c)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}I(c_{i,k}(w^{n})=c)I(w_{i}=w)}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}I(c_{i,k}(w^{n})=c)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function and the second definition is valid when the denominator is nonzero. So, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{w^{n},k}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{w^{n},k}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{w^{n},k}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{w^{n},k}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> are the empirical <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram context distribution and empirical transition distribution given a <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram context <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. When <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> are clear, we use shorthands <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{w}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{w}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{w}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{w}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the empirical distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem II.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a test sequence <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram language model <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(\textsf{PPL}(\hat{p}_{M},v^{T}))=\sum_{c\text{ in }v^{T}}p_{v}(c)\,H(p_{v}(\cdot|c))\\
+\sum_{c\text{ in }v^{T}}p_{v}(c)\,d_{\textsf{KL}}(p_{v}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,\hat{p}_{M}(\cdot|c))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">PPL</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> in </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.340em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.062em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(\textsf{PPL}(\hat{p}_{M},v^{T}))=\sum_{c\text{ in }v^{T}}p_{v}(c)\,H(p_{v}(\cdot|c))\\
+\sum_{c\text{ in }v^{T}}p_{v}(c)\,d_{\textsf{KL}}(p_{v}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,\hat{p}_{M}(\cdot|c)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p)</annotation></semantics></math> is the entropy of a distribution <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{\textsf{KL}}(p\,\|\,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\textsf{KL}}(p\,\|\,q)</annotation></semantics></math> is the KL divergence between the distributions <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. Both the KL divergence and the entropy are in base <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The decomposition in (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Theorem II.1 ‣ II-B Test perplexity and its decomposition ‣ II Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is a purely algebraic result for the perplexity of a fixed test sequence with the first empirical conditional entropy term being independent of the model <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> (this term will be significant only for very short contexts), and the second conditional KL-divergence term measuring the closeness of the model’s conditional distributions given a context to that of the corresponding empirical test distributions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Distribution estimation under KL Loss</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An important consequence of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem II.1 ‣ II-B Test perplexity and its decomposition ‣ II Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.1</span></a> is the following: for achieving low empirical perplexity, the quantity <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{\textsf{KL}}(p_{v}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,\hat{p}_{M}(\cdot|c))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>M</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\textsf{KL}}(p_{v}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,\hat{p}_{M}(\cdot|c))</annotation></semantics></math> is to be minimized and this minimization can be done independently for each context <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. This leads to the classical problem of distribution estimation under KL loss using the training corpus <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, since the test perplexity is with respect to a different test sequence <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, a smoothing method becomes necessary.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Embeddings, Semantic Similarity and Proximity in KL Distance</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Word and context embeddings</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern LLMs embed words and contexts into a real vector space. A <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of words from a vocabulary <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping taking <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Embeddings are learned such that closeness of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{\tilde{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{\tilde{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the embedding space indicates semantic connections between the words <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. The context of a word <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> occurring in the sequence <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{1},w_{2},\ldots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{1},w_{2},\ldots)</annotation></semantics></math> is either the one-sided <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram context defined earlier, or the two-sided <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram context <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w_{i-k+1},\ldots,w_{i-1},w_{i+1},\ldots,w_{i+k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w_{i-k+1},\ldots,w_{i-1},w_{i+1},\ldots,w_{i+k-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Popular embeddings such as Word2Vec and GloVe can be understood as approximate, low-rank factorizations of the Pointwise Mutual Information (PMI) matrix derived from frequency counts of words occurring in contexts of a training corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a context <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|c|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|c|</annotation></semantics></math> words, a <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping taking the embeddings of the words in the context <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="E_{c}=(e_{w}:w\in c)\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times|c|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{c}=(e_{w}:w\in c)\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times|c|}</annotation></semantics></math> to a vector <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each column of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is a length-<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> embedding of a word in the context <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, transformers (the main components of LLMs) use the idea of <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attention</span> to transform <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> (and positional encodings of words) in multiple computation steps into <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{1},\ldots,y_{|c|})\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times|c|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{1},\ldots,y_{|c|})\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times|c|}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The vector corresponding to the last context position <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{|c|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{|c|}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be the embedding <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for a context <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In general, word and context embeddings are learned using training data and satisfy some semantic relationship heuristics. We will assume such embeddings are available and use them to impose Lipschitzness conditions on the natural language process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Embeddings to next-word probabilities: Lipschitzness</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In transformer-based LLMs, the context embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is converted into <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">logits</span> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}_{w}f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}_{w}f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding of a word <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transpose of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. The logits are converted into a distribution over the words in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> using <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">softmax</span>, which converts a vector <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[a_{1},a_{2},\ldots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[a_{1},a_{2},\ldots]</annotation></semantics></math> into a probability distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Pr}(i)=\exp(a_{i})/\sum_{i}\exp(a_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(i)=\exp(a_{i})/\sum_{i}\exp(a_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
