fset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 463.77 11.81)"><path d="M 5.11 0 C 4.48 0.15 1.72 1.03 0 1.99 L 0 -1.99 C 1.72 -1.03 4.48 -0.15 5.11 0 Z"></path></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 412.64 -31.5 L 458.82 -31.5"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 458.82 -31.5)"><path d="M 5.11 0 C 4.48 0.15 1.72 1.03 0 1.99 L 0 -1.99 C 1.72 -1.03 4.48 -0.15 5.11 0 Z"></path></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Schematic depicting a language model - from embeddings to next-word probabilities.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S3.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ III-B Embeddings to next-word probabilities: Lipschitzness ‣ III Embeddings, Semantic Similarity and Proximity in KL Distance ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the schematic of a language model that uses word and context embeddings that are converted to next-word probabilities using logit-softmax.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the following generalized Lipschitz-logit model for the natural language process that generates the training corpus <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and the test sequence <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefn1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefn1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefn1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> (<math id="Thmdefn1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz-logit process)</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefn1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefn1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A random process on words in a vocabulary <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> with a context defined for each word and a transition probability <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> for a context <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz-logit if</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Pr}(w|c)=\frac{\exp(g_{w}(f_{c})+\epsilon_{c,w})}{\sum_{y\in V}\exp(g_{y}(f_{c})+\epsilon_{c,y})},\quad|\epsilon_{c,w}|\leq\epsilon\,\forall(w,c)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(w|c)=\frac{\exp(g_{w}(f_{c})+\epsilon_{c,w})}{\sum_{y\in V}\exp(g_{y}(f_{c})+\epsilon_{c,y})},\quad|\epsilon_{c,w}|\leq\epsilon\,\forall(w,c),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefn1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefn1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding for the context <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, the function <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}:\mathbb{R}^{t}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}:\mathbb{R}^{t}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz satisfying <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|g_{w}(f_{c})-g_{w}(f_{\tilde{c}})|\leq L\|f_{c}-f_{\tilde{c}}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|g_{w}(f_{c})-g_{w}(f_{\tilde{c}})|\leq L\|f_{c}-f_{\tilde{c}}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmdefn1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{c,w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{c,w}</annotation></semantics></math> is the permitted error.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As pointed out earlier, text generated by transformer-based LLMs clearly satisfy the Lipschitz-logit condition with <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}(f_{c})=e^{\prime}_{w}f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}(f_{c})=e^{\prime}_{w}f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\max_{w}\|e_{w}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\max_{w}\|e_{w}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Though our proposed smoothing method is motivated by the Lipschitz-logit assumption, we do not require any knowledge or use of the functions <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may not be available or may be computationally intensive to evaluate. Compared to the number of parameters in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> of modern LLMs, the size of the embeddings is significantly smaller.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">KL proximity</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a Lipschitz-logit process, as shown in the theorem below, closeness in context embeddings results in closeness in the KL–divergences between the corresponding conditional probability distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem III.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz-logit process, let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math> be two contexts and let their embeddings be <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tilde{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tilde{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{\textsf{KL}}(\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)\|\text{Pr}(\cdot|\tilde{c}))\leq 2L\|f_{c}-f_{\tilde{c}}\|_{2}+4\epsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\textsf{KL}}(\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)\|\text{Pr}(\cdot|\tilde{c}))\leq 2L\|f_{c}-f_{\tilde{c}}\|_{2}+4\epsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We have chosen the 2-norm in the statement of the above theorem for simplicity. A similar result will hold for all other equivalent norms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In several practical examples, the embedding function <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{t\times|c|}\to\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{t\times|c|}\to\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be shown to be Lipschitz, i.e. <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f_{c}-f_{\tilde{c}}\|_{2}\leq L_{f}\|E_{c}-E_{\tilde{c}}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f_{c}-f_{\tilde{c}}\|_{2}\leq L_{f}\|E_{c}-E_{\tilde{c}}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Frobenius norm. In Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Common Embeddings ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, we prove this for the Word2Vec Continuous-Bag-Of-Words (CBOW) model, transformer-based models (with bounded input norms) and GloVe-based bigram models. In such cases, the KL proximity of the next-word probabilities can be expressed in terms of word embeddings. This is shown in the following corollary to Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem III.1 ‣ III-C KL proximity ‣ III Embeddings, Semantic Similarity and Proximity in KL Distance ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary III.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the context embedding function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz as defined above, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="d_{\textsf{KL}}(\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)\|\text{Pr}(\cdot|\tilde{c}))\leq 2LL_{f}\|E_{c}-E_{\tilde{c}}\|_{F}+4\epsilon" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\textsf{KL}}(\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)\|\text{Pr}(\cdot|\tilde{c}))\leq 2LL_{f}\|E_{c}-E_{\tilde{c}}\|_{F}+4\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for an <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz-logit process.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The results in this and the previous section firmly establish the theoretical basis of semantic smoothing, i.e. estimation of the conditional distribution given a context under the metric of KL loss with the knowledge that the conditional distributions of semantically similar contexts are proximal in KL distance. Next, we consider such distribution estimation problems and derive minimax results for them.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{n}=(X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{n}=(X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> from a distribution <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=(\pi_{1},\pi_{2},\dots,\pi_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=(\pi_{1},\pi_{2},\dots,\pi_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=\sum_{j\in[n]}I(X_{j}=i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=\sum_{j\in[n]}I(X_{j}=i)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the individual letter counts. Let <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(\textsf{em})}_{i}=\frac{N_{i}}{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">em</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(\textsf{em})}_{i}=\frac{N_{i}}{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the empirical distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the distribution estimation problem of estimating <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> from the samples <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> under expected KL loss, minimax results have been studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and a minimax optimal estimator is the variable add-constant estimator <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">opt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}</annotation></semantics></math> that assigns <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}(i)\propto r+\beta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}(i)\propto r+\beta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to the letter <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=r</annotation></semantics></math> and the constant <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> varies with <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> as specified in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. The result is captured in the theorem below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem IV.1</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\hat{\pi}}\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi}(X^{n}))]\\
=\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}(X^{n}))]=\frac{d-1}{2n}+o\left(\frac{1}{n}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>π</mi></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>π</mi></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">opt</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\hat{\pi}}\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi}(X^{n}))]\\
=\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}(X^{n}))]=\frac{d-1}{2n}+o\left(\frac{1}{n}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is an estimator for the distribution <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and the expectation is over the samples <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{n}\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{n}\sim</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Minimax distribution estimation with side information</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider the scenario where, along with the i.i.d. samples <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{n}\sim\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{n}\sim\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, we have side information that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">close</span> to another distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}=(\pi^{(0)}_{1},\pi^{(0)}_{2},\dots,\pi^{(0)}_{d_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}=(\pi^{(0)}_{1},\pi^{(0)}_{2},\dots,\pi^{(0)}_{d_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>. More precisely, our side information is that the distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)=\{\pi^{\prime}:d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi^{\prime}\|\pi^{(0)})\leq\Delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)=\{\pi^{\prime}:d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi^{\prime}\|\pi^{(0)})\leq\Delta\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>-neighborhood around <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> in KL distance. We assume supports of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> are such that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is finite. In terms of language models, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi=\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{(0)}=\text{Pr}(\cdot|\tilde{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext>Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}=\text{Pr}(\cdot|\tilde{c})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the semantic similarity between the two contexts <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math> evaluated through the Lipschitz-logit assumption. We will call <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> as <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">synonymous</span> distributions. For now, we assume that <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> is known exactly, and we will relax this assumption later.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The minimax distribution estimation problem with side information can be stated as</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\hat{\pi}}\max_{\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi}(X^{n}))]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></munder><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\hat{\pi}}\max_{\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi}(X^{n}))],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the expectation is over the samples <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{n}\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{n}\sim</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. This specific problem with expected KL loss and KL proximity has not been considered in earlier work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our first result for the minimax problem in (<a href="#S4.E9" title="In IV-A Minimax distribution estimation with side information ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) is the following upper bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem IV.2</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The linear interpolation estimator <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}=\alpha\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}(X^{n})+(1-\alpha)\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}=\alpha\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}(X^{n})+(1-\alpha)\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> for appropriate values of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> achieves</span></p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi})]\leq\min\left(\frac{d-1}{2n}+o\left(\frac{1}{n}\right),\Delta\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">KL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>o</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi})]\leq\min\left(\frac{d-1}{2n}+o\left(\frac{1}{n}\right),\Delta\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the expectation is over the samples <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{n}\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{n}\sim</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our second result is a matching lower bound for a special case of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem IV.3</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}=\Unif[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}=\Unif[d]</annotation></semantics></math> be the uniform distribution on an alphabet of size <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Then,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\hat{\pi}}\max_{\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi})]\geq\frac{1}{32}\min\left(\frac{d}{4n},\frac{1}{4},\Delta\right)e^{-4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></munder><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">KL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>32</mn></mfrac><mi>min</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\hat{\pi}}\max_{\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)}\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi})]\geq\frac{1}{32}\min\left(\frac{d}{4n},\frac{1}{4},\Delta\right)e^{-4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta&lt;1/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta&lt;1/4</annotation></semantics></math>, it can be seen that the lower bound for uniform <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and upper bound are both <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta\left(\min\left(\Delta,\frac{d}{n}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta\left(\min\left(\Delta,\frac{d}{n}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Extension to <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> synonyms</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results above are stated for one synonymous distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to see that if there are <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> synonymous distributions <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(1)},\pi^{(2)}\dots\pi^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(1)},\pi^{(2)}\dots\pi^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> with respective KL proximities <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{1},\Delta_{2},\dots,\Delta_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{1},\Delta_{2},\dots,\Delta_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the linear interpolation estimator</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{0}\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}+\alpha_{1}\pi^{(1)}+\alpha_{2}\pi^{(2)}+\dots+\alpha_{m}\pi^{(m)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">opt</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{0}\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}+\alpha_{1}\pi^{(1)}+\alpha_{2}\pi^{(2)}+\dots+\alpha_{m}\pi^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=0}^{m}\alpha_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=0}^{m}\alpha_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the upper bound of</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O\left(\min\left(\frac{d-1}{2n},\Delta_{1},\Delta_{2},\dots,\Delta_{m}\right)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O\left(\min\left(\frac{d-1}{2n},\Delta_{1},\Delta_{2},\dots,\Delta_{m}\right)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The bound above is minimized by placing all mass on the source with the smallest loss. In practice, we use a softmin (or other similar functions) over the estimated loss proxies to obtain a more stable interpolation as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}\propto\begin{cases}\exp(-\tau(\frac{d-1}{2n})),&amp;i=0,\\
\exp(-\tau\Delta_{i}),&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}\propto\begin{cases}\exp(-\tau(\frac{d-1}{2n})),&amp;i=0,\\
\exp(-\tau\Delta_{i}),&amp;\text{otherwise},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature parameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Estimated synonymous distribution</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">So far, we have assumed that the synonymous distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> is known completely. However, in the context of language models, the distributions <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> arise as distributions <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{Pr}(\cdot|\tilde{c})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(\cdot|\tilde{c})</annotation></semantics></math> following similar contexts <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, in practice, we will not directly know <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, but we will have, say, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> samples from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of times the context <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{c}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in the training sample, and we have to estimate the distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this version of the problem of estimating <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{n}\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{n}\sim</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=(\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=(\pi_{1},\ldots,\pi_{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{n_{0}}\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msup><mo>∼</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{n_{0}}\sim</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}=(\pi^{(0)}_{1},\ldots,\pi^{(0)}_{d_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}=(\pi^{(0)}_{1},\ldots,\pi^{(0)}_{d_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math> with the side information that <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\in B_{\text{KL}}(\pi^{(0)},\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math>. We proceed with a <span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">plugin</span> approach as follows. First, from the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we find the variable add-constant estimate <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}^{(0)}_{\textsf{opt}}(Y^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">opt</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}^{(0)}_{\textsf{opt}}(Y^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is minimax optimal as claimed in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem IV.1 ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV.1</span></a>. Next, we use <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}^{(0)}_{\textsf{opt}}(Y^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">opt</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}^{(0)}_{\textsf{opt}}(Y^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> in place of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> in the linear interpolation estimator in Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem IV.2 ‣ IV-A Minimax distribution estimation with side information ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV.2</span></a> to obtain an estimator for <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. For this plugin-interpolated estimator we prove a risk upper bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem IV.4</span></span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The estimator</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}=\alpha\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}(X^{n})+(1-\alpha)\hat{\pi}^{(0)}_{\textsf{opt}}(Y^{n})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">opt</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">opt</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}=\alpha\hat{\pi}_{\textsf{opt}}(X^{n})+(1-\alpha)\hat{\pi}^{(0)}_{\textsf{opt}}(Y^{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for appropriate values of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi})]\leq\min\Bigg(\frac{d-1}{2n}+o\left(\frac{d}{n}\right),\\
\Delta+\log\left(1+\frac{d_{0}}{n_{0}}\right)+\log\left(2\right)\Bigg)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif-italic">KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>min</mi><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mi>o</mi><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(\pi\|\hat{\pi})]\leq\min\Bigg(\frac{d-1}{2n}+o\left(\frac{d}{n}\right),\\
\Delta+\log\left(1+\frac{d_{0}}{n_{0}}\right)+\log\left(2\right)\Bigg).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For typical parameters, if the approximation <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(1+x)\approx x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(1+x)\approx x</annotation></semantics></math> holds, we get a rough bound of the order of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\left(\frac{d}{n},\Delta+\frac{d_{0}}{n_{0}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\left(\frac{d}{n},\Delta+\frac{d_{0}}{n_{0}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. As before, the estimator and bound of Theorem <a href="#S4.E15" title="In Theorem IV.4 ‣ IV-C Estimated synonymous distribution ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> can be extended analogously from a single synonym to <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> synonyms.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Semantic Smoothing: Theory to Practice</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a corpus with training sequence <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}=(u_{1},\ldots,u_{S})</annotation></semantics></math> and test sequence <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{T}=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{T}=(v_{1},\ldots,v_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, which we will assume are generated through a common random process <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> that is <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz-logit with next-word probabilities <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> for a context <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, embeddings <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> for a word <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> for the context <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. The embeddings are assumed to be available along with <math id="S5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for training a language model <math id="S5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta(M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta(M)</annotation></semantics></math> and obtain the next-word probabilities <math id="S5.p1.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> so as to minimize the test perplexity <math id="S5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsf{PPL}(\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}},v^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">PPL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsf{PPL}(\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}},v^{T})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretically, we assume random <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{S}\sim\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{S}\sim\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{T}\sim\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{T}\sim\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and consider the following problem of minimizing the expected log perplexity:</p>
<table id="S5.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\theta(M)}\mathbb{E}_{U^{S},V^{T}}[\log(\textsf{PPL}(\hat{p}_{M,U^{S}},V^{T})]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">PPL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta(M)}\mathbb{E}_{U^{S},V^{T}}[\log(\textsf{PPL}(\hat{p}_{M,U^{S}},V^{T})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Using the decomposition of perplexity in Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem II.1 ‣ II-B Test perplexity and its decomposition ‣ II Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.1</span></a> (with suitable separability assumptions for context stationary probabilities) and the proximity results of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem III.1 ‣ III-C KL proximity ‣ III Embeddings, Semantic Similarity and Proximity in KL Distance ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></a> (and its corollaries), we relate the problem to the following distribution estimation with side information problem for each context <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\min_{\theta(M)}\mathbb{E}_{U^{S}}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,p_{M,U^{S}}(\cdot|c)]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta(M)}\mathbb{E}_{U^{S}}[d_{\textsf{KL}}(p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c)\,\|\,p_{M,U^{S}}(\cdot|c)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with the side information that <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> is close in KL distance to <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,m</annotation></semantics></math>, where the <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are neighboring or synonymous contexts in the embedding space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For a specific training sequence <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{S}=u^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{S}=u^{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, the language model <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> estimates <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{P}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the ideas of Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="IV-C Estimated synonymous distribution ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-C</span></a> and, in particular, Theorem <a href="#S4.E15" title="In Theorem IV.4 ‣ IV-C Estimated synonymous distribution ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, we define the semantically smoothed version of the language model’s estimate, denoted <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{p}^{\text{sem}}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow><mtext>sem</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}^{\text{sem}}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{p}^{\text{sem}}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow><mtext>sem</mtext></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{p}^{\text{sem}}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\alpha^{(c)}_{c}\,\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\alpha^{(c)}_{c}\,\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle+\alpha^{(c)}_{c_{1}}\,\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c_{1})+\cdots+\alpha^{(c)}_{c_{m}}\,\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c_{m})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\alpha^{(c)}_{c_{1}}\,\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c_{1})+\cdots+\alpha^{(c)}_{c_{m}}\,\hat{p}_{M,u^{S}}(\cdot|c_{m}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{(c)}_{c},\alpha^{(c)}_{c_{i}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(c)}_{c},\alpha^{(c)}_{c_{i}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\ldots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\ldots,m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{(c)}_{c}+\sum_{i=1}^{m}\alpha^{(c)}_{c_{i}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(c)}_{c}+\sum_{i=1}^{m}\alpha^{(c)}_{c_{i}}=1</annotation></semantics></math> are linear interpolation coefficients that are treated as hyperparameters and chosen using ideas motivated by the theoretical bounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose the context <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> occurs <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> times in <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the context <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> appears <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> times. To use the bounds of Theorem <a href="#S4.E15" title="In Theorem IV.4 ‣ IV-C Estimated synonymous distribution ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, we require knowledge of the alphabet size <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> of words that follow <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and the alphabet size <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{c_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{c_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> of words that follow <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. In experiments, when the sizes are unknown, we estimate them using the samples of next words following the respective contexts in <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u^{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>S</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u^{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we estimate the KL proximity of <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> using Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem III.1 ‣ III-C KL proximity ‣ III Embeddings, Semantic Similarity and Proximity in KL Distance ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III.1</span></a> as</p>
<table id="S5.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{cc_{i}}=L\|f_{c}-f_{c_{i}}\|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{cc_{i}}=L\|f_{c}-f_{c_{i}}\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p4.2" class="ltx_p">for a suitable norm (usually <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> norm) and a hyperparameter Lipschitz constant <math id="S5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> chosen suitably.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Applying the ideas in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Extension to m synonyms ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>, we pick <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{(c)}_{c^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{(c)}_{c^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> as being proportional to a function of the respective KL loss proxies (upper bounds from Theorem <a href="#S4.E15" title="In Theorem IV.4 ‣ IV-C Estimated synonymous distribution ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) as follows (this requires a mild generalization of the proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.E15" title="In Theorem IV.4 ‣ IV-C Estimated synonymous distribution ‣ IV Distribution Estimation under KL Loss with KL Proximity Side Information ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> for an arbitrary <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha^{(c)}_{c^{\prime}}\propto\begin{cases}\phi\left(\frac{d_{c}-1}{2n_{c}}\right),&amp;c^{\prime}=c,\\
\phi\left(\Delta_{cc^{\prime}}+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta d_{c^{\prime}}}{n_{c^{\prime}}}\right)+[\log(\frac{1}{\beta})]_{+}\right),&amp;c^{\prime}=c_{i},\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha^{(c)}_{c^{\prime}}\propto\begin{cases}\phi\left(\frac{d_{c}-1}{2n_{c}}\right),&amp;c^{\prime}=c,\\
\phi\left(\Delta_{cc^{\prime}}+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta d_{c^{\prime}}}{n_{c^{\prime}}}\right)+[\log(\frac{1}{\beta})]_{+}\right),&amp;c^{\prime}=c_{i},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)_{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)_{+}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(x,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(x,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and the function <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> could be chosen as <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp(-\tau x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp(-\tau x)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\softmin" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">softmin</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\softmin</annotation></semantics></math> or another similar one. For any language model <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, we will use the loss proxies of an add-<math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in (<a href="#S5.E20" title="In V Semantic Smoothing: Theory to Practice ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For any language model <math id="S5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the above idea of semantic smoothing can be applied to obtain new estimates of next-word probabilities. Theoretically, this approach is motivated by the minimization of expected test perplexity under the assumptions described in the sections above. To verify the theory, we provide experimental results with both a synthetic and a natural language data set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Synthetic experiments on Markov data</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume a bigram model and generate the train and test sequences by a Markov process with transition matrix <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{cw}=\text{Pr}(w|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{cw}=\text{Pr}(w|c)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the context which is the previous word and <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the word following the context. The Markov process has <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V|=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V|=100</annotation></semantics></math> states and the transition matrix has <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> distinct rows repeated <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> times each where the distinct rows are randomly generated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The embeddings are also <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=10</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional vectors.
The embeddings are obtained by factorizing the matrix <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{cw}=\log\left(\frac{\text{Pr}(w|c)}{\text{Pr}(c)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{cw}=\log\left(\frac{\text{Pr}(w|c)}{\text{Pr}(c)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{cw}=e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{cw}=e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the transpose of <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>). Such a factorization exists since <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is low rank. So,</p>
<table id="S6.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exp(e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{w})=\frac{\text{Pr}(w|c)}{\text{Pr}(c)},\quad\sum_{y\in V_{c}}\exp(e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{y})=\frac{1}{P(c)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.000em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exp(e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{w})=\frac{\text{Pr}(w|c)}{\text{Pr}(c)},\quad\sum_{y\in V_{c}}\exp(e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{y})=\frac{1}{P(c)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Pr}(w|c)=\frac{\exp(e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{w})}{\sum_{y\in V_{c}}\exp(e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{y})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Pr}(w|c)=\frac{\exp(e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{w})}{\sum_{y\in V_{c}}\exp(e^{\prime}_{c}\tilde{e}_{y})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which is <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,0)</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz-logit with <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}(x)=x^{\prime}\tilde{e}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}(x)=x^{\prime}\tilde{e}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\max_{w\in V}\|\tilde{e}_{w}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\max_{w\in V}\|\tilde{e}_{w}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07994v1/synthetic_simulations_error_bars.svg" id="S6.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:23.305em; height:17.082em;aspect-ratio:310/227;" width="310" height="227"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Perplexity vs Number of Training samples for semantic smoothing with varying number of synonyms.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The add-<math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> model uses</p>
<table id="S6.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{\text{add}-\beta}(w|c)=\frac{N_{c,w}+\beta}{N_{c}+\beta|V|}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>add</mtext><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{\text{add}-\beta}(w|c)=\frac{N_{c,w}+\beta}{N_{c}+\beta|V|},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{c,w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{c,w}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of times the word <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> occurs after the context <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in the training data, <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{c}=\sum_{w\in V}N_{c,w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{c}=\sum_{w\in V}N_{c,w}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of occurrences of the unigram <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in the training data, and <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the add-<math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> estimator with <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> and its semantically smoothed versions with <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\{5,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\{5,10\}</annotation></semantics></math> synonyms that are the words closest to the context word in the embedding space. The softmin function with <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> is used for interpolation. We take <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\textsf{test}}=5\times 10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_sans-serif">test</mtext></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\textsf{test}}=5\times 10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> samples for computing test perplexity with <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> repetitions for obtaining error bars. A plot of test perplexity vs the number of training samples <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ VI-A Synthetic experiments on Markov data ‣ VI Experiments ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It is seen that the test perplexity improves significantly by semantic smoothing. The semantic-smoothed estimators perform better than the naive add-constant, Kneser-Ney and Jelinek-Mercer estimators, and come close to the conditional entropy lower bound <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(p(\text{word}|\text{context}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>word</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mtext>context</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p(\text{word}|\text{context}))</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem II.1 ‣ II-B Test perplexity and its decomposition ‣ II Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.1</span></a> even for small values of <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experiments on natural language data</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the semantic smoothing method on natural language data to show its effectiveness even when there is no simple bigram process for data generation. Our goal is to merely show the effectiveness of semantic smoothing and no effort has been made to optimize for the lowest perplexities by hyperparameter tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset, embeddings and preprocessing</span>: We run experiments on the WikiText-103 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, using the inbuilt train-test split as available in the Python Datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> module. The validation data is not used. A token (typically a word) is defined to be the lowest level of division of a sentence. The data is first preprocessed by i) making letters lowercase, ii) replacing numbers with a <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;num&gt;</span> token and URLs with a <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;url&gt;</span> token, iii) removing references, non-ASCII characters and punctuation, and iv) normalizing whitespaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the simulations, we use Word2Vec (<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, GloVe (<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and GPT-2 (<math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="768" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>768</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">768</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> embeddings. In the case of Word2Vec and GloVe embeddings, tokens are created by i) splitting the sentences at whitespaces, and ii) padding the beginning and end of each sentence with <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;bos&gt;</span> and <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;eos&gt;</span> tokens, respectively. For GPT-2 embeddings, we use the GPT-2 tokenizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. We construct a corpus consisting of <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100,000</annotation></semantics></math> training sentences and <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10,000</annotation></semantics></math> test sentences from Wikitext-103 after preprocessing and sentence tokenization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be the vocabulary, defined formally to be the set of unique tokens appearing in either the train or the test sequence. Using standard word-level tokenization, the vocabulary size is found to be <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="66,753" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>66,753</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">66,753</annotation></semantics></math>. For GPT2 tokenization, the preprocessing pipeline produces a reduced vocabulary size of <math id="S6.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="27,007" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>27,007</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">27,007</annotation></semantics></math>, due to byte-pair encoding.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Language models</span>: We again consider bigram language models, such as the add-<math id="S6.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> estimator defined earlier and the Kneser-Ney (KN) model. The KN model with an absolute discount <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, for a context <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> which is the
previous word and the next word <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as</p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}_{\text{KN}-D}(w|c)=\frac{\max(N_{c,w}{-}D,0)}{N_{c}}+\lambda_{c}P_{\text{cont}}(c)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>KN</mtext><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}_{\text{KN}-D}(w|c)=\frac{\max(N_{c,w}{-}D,0)}{N_{c}}+\lambda_{c}P_{\text{cont}}(c),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{w}=D\,N_{1}^{+}(w,\cdot)/N_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{w}=D\,N_{1}^{+}(w,\cdot)/N_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{cont}}(w)=N_{1}^{+}(\cdot,w)/N_{1}^{+}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>cont</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{cont}}(w)=N_{1}^{+}(\cdot,w)/N_{1}^{+}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}^{+}(w,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}^{+}(w,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of unique continuations after <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}^{+}(\cdot,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}^{+}(\cdot,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of unique contexts before <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}^{+}(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}^{+}(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of unique bigrams.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic smoothing</span>: For a bigram model, the context <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is a word <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and the embedding <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> equals the word embedding <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. We compute the KL proximity as <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{w\tilde{w}}=5\|e_{w}-e_{\tilde{w}}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{w\tilde{w}}=5\|e_{w}-e_{\tilde{w}}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where we have chosen the Lipschitz constant as <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> based on the maximum norm of the word embeddings. For each <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, we estimate the alphabet size of the words following <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{d}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{d}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, using the Chebyshev polynomial estimator from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. We pick the <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> synonymous words of a given word <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to be the <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> words <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math> with the smallest values of <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{cc^{\prime}}+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta\hat{d}_{c^{\prime}}}{n_{c^{\prime}}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{cc^{\prime}}+\log\left(1+\frac{\beta\hat{d}_{c^{\prime}}}{n_{c^{\prime}}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, choosing <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.005</annotation></semantics></math>. The interpolation function <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is set to be <math id="S6.SS2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)=1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)=1/x</annotation></semantics></math>. For any word in the train or test sequence that does not have an embedding, we skip semantic smoothing.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07994v1/combined_add_constants_new.svg" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:23.772em; height:17.136em;aspect-ratio:316/228;" width="316" height="228"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Perplexity vs no. of synonyms for add-<math id="S6.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> model. The conditional entropy lower bound from Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem II.1 ‣ II-B Test perplexity and its decomposition ‣ II Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.1</span></a> is <math id="S6.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.51" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>33.51</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.51</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For the add-constant model, a plot of test perplexity versus number of synonyms of semantic smoothing using GloVe and Word2Vec embeddings is shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ VI-B Experiments on natural language data ‣ VI Experiments ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We can see that the test perplexity drops as the number of synonyms increases for different constants <math id="S6.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07994v1/combined_kn_discounts_new.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:23.772em; height:17.511em;aspect-ratio:316/233;" width="316" height="233"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Perplexity vs no. of synonyms for Kneser-Ney model. The conditional entropy lower bound from Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem II.1 ‣ II-B Test perplexity and its decomposition ‣ II Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.1</span></a> is <math id="S6.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.51" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>33.51</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.51</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A similar plot for the Kneser-Ney model is shown in Fig. <a href="#S6.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VI-B Experiments on natural language data ‣ VI Experiments ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and an improvement in perplexity is observed with increasing <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> for different values of the absolute discount <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Comparing Figs. <a href="#S6.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ VI-B Experiments on natural language data ‣ VI Experiments ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S6.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ VI-B Experiments on natural language data ‣ VI Experiments ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we observe that the absolute perplexities are significantly better for the KN model and that semantic smoothing (<math id="S6.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=50</annotation></semantics></math>) is effective in both cases in different proportions – about <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="700" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>700</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">700</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="520" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>520</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">520</annotation></semantics></math> for add-<math id="S6.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.003" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.003</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.003</annotation></semantics></math> model and about <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="310" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>310</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">310</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="295" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>295</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">295</annotation></semantics></math> for KN-<math id="S6.SS2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07994v1/gpt2_only_4styles_new.svg" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:23.772em; height:17.136em;aspect-ratio:316/228;" width="316" height="228"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>Perplexity vs no. of synonyms for add-<math id="S6.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and KN-<math id="S6.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> models using GPT-2 embeddings.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in Fig. <a href="#S6.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ VI-B Experiments on natural language data ‣ VI Experiments ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we plot perplexity versus number of synonyms when using GPT-2 embeddings and tokenization, which results in a significantly lower absolute perplexity value. However, semantic smoothing (<math id="S6.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=50</annotation></semantics></math>) results in improvements – about <math id="S6.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="240" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>240</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">240</annotation></semantics></math> to under <math id="S6.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>200</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">200</annotation></semantics></math> for add-<math id="S6.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.003" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.003</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.003</annotation></semantics></math> and about <math id="S6.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="164" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>164</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">164</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="160" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>160</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">160</annotation></semantics></math> for KN-<math id="S6.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, while several improvements are potentially possible, these initial experiments with both synthetic and natural language data establish the effectiveness of semantic smoothing for reducing the perplexity metric in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Concluding remarks</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We proposed semantic smoothing, a framework for improving language-model probability estimates by combining information across semantically similar contexts. The method has information-theoretic backing through a distribution estimation with side information problem. The theoretical and experimental results suggest that embedding geometry can provide useful smoothing information beyond frequency counts alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Several directions remain open. First, the present experiments focus on bigram models; extending the method to longer contexts requires scalable nearest-neighbor search over context embeddings and careful estimation of the corresponding interpolation weights. Second, the current weighting rules are motivated by minimax upper bounds but are not optimized end-to-end for held-out perplexity; data-dependent or adaptive weighting may further improve performance. Third, it will be interesting to train/use embeddings optimized for the purpose of semantic smoothing as opposed to using general purpose ones. Finally, the distribution-estimation problem with side information appears to be of independent interest, particularly when the side-information distribution is itself estimated from samples. Several aspects of this problem including optimal minimax rates for a general side information distribution remain open.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VIII </span><span id="S8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proofs of Theorems</span></h2>

<section id="S8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S8.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">VIII-A</span> </span><span id="S8.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem II.1 ‣ II-B Test perplexity and its decomposition ‣ II Language Models: Perplexity Minimization and KL Loss ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II.1</span></a></span></h3>

<div id="S8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{c,w}=\sum_{i=1}^{T}I(c_{i,k}(v^{T})=c)I(v_{i}=w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{c,w}=\sum_{i=1}^{T}I(c_{i,k}(v^{T})=c)I(v_{i}=w)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the number of times <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> occurs after a <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gram context <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>v</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that</p>
<table id="S8.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

