<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Common Embeddings</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many models for language used while training embeddings satisfy the <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz-logit process defined in (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In Definition 1 ((𝐿,ϵ)-Lipschitz-logit process) ‣ III-B Embeddings to next-word probabilities: Lipschitzness ‣ III Embeddings, Semantic Similarity and Proximity in KL Distance ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="A1.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">A-A</span> </span><span id="A1.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Word2Vec</span></h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Word2Vec ‘input’ embedding of each word <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times|c|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times|c|}</annotation></semantics></math> is the input context embedding matrix. Under the Continuous Bag of Words (CBOW) model, this is converted to the final context embedding <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}\in\mathbb{R}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by averaging the individual word embeddings, i.e.</p>
<table id="A1.E43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}=\frac{1}{k}E_{c}1_{|c|\times 1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}=\frac{1}{k}E_{c}1_{|c|\times 1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{|c|\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{|c|\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|c|\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|c|\times 1</annotation></semantics></math> matrix of all ones. This process is Lipschitz with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=\frac{\sqrt{|c|}}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msqrt><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=\frac{\sqrt{|c|}}{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The probability estimates in the model are trained to be</p>
<table id="A1.E44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Pr}(w|c)=\frac{\exp(f_{c}^{T}\tilde{e}_{w}+\epsilon_{c,w})}{\sum_{y\in V}\exp(f_{c}^{T}\tilde{e}_{y}+\epsilon_{c,y})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(w|c)=\frac{\exp(f_{c}^{T}\tilde{e}_{w}+\epsilon_{c,w})}{\sum_{y\in V}\exp(f_{c}^{T}\tilde{e}_{y}+\epsilon_{c,y})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{e}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{e}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are ‘output’ embeddings of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{c,w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{c,w}</annotation></semantics></math> includes possible training error in the model. The function <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}(x)=x^{\prime}\tilde{e}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}(x)=x^{\prime}\tilde{e}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz for <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\max_{w\in V}\|\tilde{e}_{w}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>e</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\max_{w\in V}\|\tilde{e}_{w}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="A1.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">A-B</span> </span><span id="A1.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Transformer-based embeddings</span></h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A transformer takes the embedding context <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> from the word embeddings <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and first converts it to <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t\times|c|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t\times|c|}</annotation></semantics></math> using the attention mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. Recall that the last context position, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{|c|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{|c|}</annotation></semantics></math>, is taken to be <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>. As before, the probabilities in the model are trained to be</p>
<table id="A1.E45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Pr}(w|c)=\frac{\exp(f_{c}^{\prime}e_{w}+\epsilon_{c,w})}{\sum_{y\in V}\exp(f_{c}^{\prime}e_{y}+\epsilon_{c,y})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Pr}(w|c)=\frac{\exp(f_{c}^{\prime}e_{w}+\epsilon_{c,w})}{\sum_{y\in V}\exp(f_{c}^{\prime}e_{y}+\epsilon_{c,y})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{c,w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{c,w}</annotation></semantics></math> includes possible training errors.
The function <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}(x)=x^{\prime}e_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}(x)=x^{\prime}e_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz for <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\max_{w\in V}\|e_{w}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\max_{w\in V}\|e_{w}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and taking <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}(y_{t\times|c|})=y_{|c|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}(y_{t\times|c|})=y_{|c|}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is left to show that the conversion of <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t\times|c|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t\times|c|}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> Lipschitz. A transformer consists of <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> sequential layers. If we prove that the <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th layer is <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz, then we get <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{f}=\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{f}=\prod_{i=1}^{\ell}L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, it is sufficient to show that the individual layers are Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self Attention</span>: Self-attention is computed as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="A1.E46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)=\text{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)V," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><mi>d</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)=\text{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d}}\right)V,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q=XW_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=XW_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=XW_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=XW_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=XW_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=XW_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Without any input constraints, the self-attention step is not Lipschitz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. However, as shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Theorem 3.3]</cite>, when the norm of the input vectors is bounded, the Lipschitz constant of self-attention scales as <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for a sequence length <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. From <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Lemma 3.8]</cite>, note that if each head is Lipschitz and the matrices have finite norm, the overall multi-headed attention block is also Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Residual Connections</span>: A layer of the form <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=x+h(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=x+h(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+L)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+L)</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz if <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz. Since <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is either a linear or a self attention layer and both of them are Lipschitz, <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Layer Normalization</span>: The LayerNorm is expressed as:</p>
<table id="A1.E47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)=\frac{x-\mu(x)}{\sqrt{\sigma^{2}(x)+\epsilon}}\,\gamma+\beta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)=\frac{x-\mu(x)}{\sqrt{\sigma^{2}(x)+\epsilon}}\,\gamma+\beta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma,\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are parameters, <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu(x)=\frac{1}{d}\sum_{i=1}^{d}x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu(x)=\frac{1}{d}\sum_{i=1}^{d}x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}(x)=\frac{1}{d}\sum_{i=1}^{d}(x_{i}-\mu(x))^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}(x)=\frac{1}{d}\sum_{i=1}^{d}(x_{i}-\mu(x))^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a small constant added for numerical stability. It is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, Supplementary material, Section A]</cite> that the LayerNorm is Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Transformer components such as MLP/feed-forward block and activation functions are known to be Lipschitz.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="A1.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">A-C</span> </span><span id="A1.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GloVe</span></h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GloVe embeddings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> are trained to ensure the following:</p>
<table id="A1.E48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(X_{\tilde{w}w})=f^{\prime}_{\tilde{w}}e_{w}+b_{\tilde{w}}+\tilde{b}_{w}+\epsilon_{\tilde{w},w}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(X_{\tilde{w}w})=f^{\prime}_{\tilde{w}}e_{w}+b_{\tilde{w}}+\tilde{b}_{w}+\epsilon_{\tilde{w},w},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\tilde{w}w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\tilde{w}w}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of times word <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> occurs as the word to be predicted when word <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is in the context <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\tilde{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\tilde{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding of the word <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math> when it acts as the context, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding of the <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> when it acts as the word to be predicted, and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\tilde{w},w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\tilde{w},w}</annotation></semantics></math> is the training error.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a bigram model, the context is the previous word in the GloVe model, i.e. <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c=\tilde{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c=\tilde{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. In such a case, the context embedding <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{c}=e_{\tilde{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{c}=e_{\tilde{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>. If sufficiently many samples are taken, due to stationarity and ergodicity, note that</p>
<table id="A1.E49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{X_{cw}}{X_{c}}\approx\text{Pr}(W_{t}=w|W_{t-1}=c)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{X_{cw}}{X_{c}}\approx\text{Pr}(W_{t}=w|W_{t-1}=c),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{c}=\sum_{y\in V_{c}}X_{cy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{c}=\sum_{y\in V_{c}}X_{cy}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Combining equations (<a href="#A1.E48" title="In A-C GloVe ‣ Appendix A Common Embeddings ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">48</span></a>) and (<a href="#A1.E49" title="In A-C GloVe ‣ Appendix A Common Embeddings ‣ Semantic Smoothing for Language Models via Distribution Estimation and Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>), we get</p>
<table id="A1.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Pr}(w|c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Pr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Pr}(w|c)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E50.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∝</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E50.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exp(f^{\prime}_{c}e_{w}+b_{c}+\tilde{b}_{w}+\epsilon_{c,w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exp(f^{\prime}_{c}e_{w}+b_{c}+\tilde{b}_{w}+\epsilon_{c,w})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∝</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\exp(f^{\prime}_{c}e_{w}+\tilde{b}_{w}+\epsilon_{c,w})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\exp(f^{\prime}_{c}e_{w}+\tilde{b}_{w}+\epsilon_{c,w}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">From the above, we observe that the next-word probabilities are Lipschitz-logit with <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}(x)=x^{\prime}e_{w}+\tilde{b}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}(x)=x^{\prime}e_{w}+\tilde{b}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz with a constant <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{w}\|e_{w}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>w</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{w}\|e_{w}\|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
